601	@@ -0,0 +1,134 @@
602	+# SMA-834: Anthropic translator keeps an open tool round whole at a tool-use-only assistant message
603	+
604	+Linear: [SMA-834](https://linear.app/smaschek/issue/SMA-834/anthropic-translator-splits-an-open-tool-round-at-a-tool-use-only)
605	+
606	+## Intent
607	+
608	+The core model has two equal forms for a tool call: a top-level
609	+`Item::ToolCall`, and a `ContentPart::ToolUse` nested inside an
610	+`Item::AssistantMessage`. In
611	+`crates/paigasus-helikon-providers-anthropic/src/translate/request.rs` the two
612	+forms give different wire orders inside an open tool round. The nested form
613	+splits the round, and the Anthropic API rejects the request because a user turn
614	+lacks a `tool_result` for a `tool_use` in the turn before it.
615	+
616	+SMA-824 fixed the same gap in the Bedrock translator (PR #365, squash commit
617	+`4003a131` on `main`). This change ports that rule.
618	+
619	+## Current behavior
620	+
621	+- The `ToolCall` arm keeps the pending results when `round_is_answered` is
622	+  `false`. It appends its `tool_use` block to the last assistant message in
623	+  `messages`, or to `pending_tool_use` when the last message is not an
624	+  assistant message.
625	+- The `AssistantMessage` arm, for a non-empty message, always flushes
626	+  `pending_tool_use` and `pending_tool_results`, then pushes a new assistant
627	+  message.
628	+
629	+Example items: `user q, assistant[ToolUse t1, ToolUse t2], ToolResult t1,
630	+assistant[ToolUse t3], ToolResult t2, ToolResult t3`.
631	+
632	+- Today: `user[q], assistant[t1, t2], user[r1], assistant[t3], user[r2, r3]`.
633	+  The API rejects this.
634	+- With the fix: `user[q], assistant[t1, t2, t3], user[r1, r2, r3]`. This is the
635	+  same wire as the `Item::ToolCall t3` form.
636	+
637	+## Goals
638	+
639	+- A non-empty `AssistantMessage` joins the open round when all three conditions
640	+  are true:
641	+  1. every block in the **output of `assistant_blocks`** is a `tool_use` block.
642	+     `assistant_blocks` already drops blank text, reasoning, and unsupported
643	+     parts, so `[Reasoning, ToolUse t3]` and `[Text " ", ToolUse t3]` join.
644	+     This matches Bedrock.
645	+  2. `pending_tool_results` is not empty,
646	+  3. `round_is_answered(&messages, &pending_tool_results)` is `false`.
647	+- "Joins" means the same thing as in the `ToolCall` arm: the pending results stay
648	+  pending, and the blocks go to the end of the last assistant message in
649	+  `messages`. One private helper does "append to the last assistant message, or
650	+  else queue in `pending_tool_use`". The `ToolCall` arm and the new branch both
651	+  call it, so the two paths cannot become different.
652	+- Two invariants make the append correct. The code comment states both:
653	+  - On the join path the last message is an assistant message.
654	+    `round_is_answered` returns `true` for an empty trailing assistant run, so
655	+    condition 3 needs at least one assistant message at the end.
656	+  - On the join path `pending_tool_use` is empty. It gets blocks only when the
657	+    last message is not an assistant message, and each `ToolResult` flushes it.
658	+    Condition 2 needs a `ToolResult` after the last queue.
659	+- `last_item_dropped` becomes `false` on that path, for symmetry with every
660	+  kept message. No test can observe it: on the join path the flag is already
661	+  `false`, because a `ToolResult` came after any dropped user message.
662	+- Assumption: an assistant message inside an open round belongs to the same
663	+  model response. The `ToolCall` arm already makes this assumption
664	+  (`interleaved_parallel_round_is_not_split`). For a log with a late result,
665	+  the fix sends a valid request in a different order instead of a rejected one.
666	+
667	+## Non-goals
668	+
669	+- No change for an answered round, for no pending results, or for an empty
670	+  message. These keep their current behavior.
671	+- **Known gap, kept as is:** a text-only or mixed (text plus tool use) assistant
672	+  message inside an open round still flushes. The nested form and the
673	+  top-level form (`AssistantMessage(text)` then `ToolCall`, as `build_items`
674	+  emits) both give a request that the API rejects. Bedrock has the same gap
675	+  (`assistant_message_flushes_calls_before_results`). This goes to a follow-up
676	+  issue for both translators.
677	+- No de-duplication of tool-use ids. `asst[t1, t2], r1, asst[tu t1]` gives
678	+  `asst[t1, t2, t1]`, which the API rejects. Today's output is also rejected,
679	+  and the `ToolCall` arm does not de-duplicate either.
680	+- No change to `round_is_answered`, `user_blocks`, or `assistant_blocks`.
681	+- No change to other providers. Bedrock is already fixed. The OpenAI, LiteLLM,
682	+  and Gemini translators are out of scope.
683	+
684	+## Anti-goals
685	+
686	+- No change to the wire for a request that the API accepts today. The new
687	+  branch runs only when today's output already has a user turn that lacks a
688	+  result. The existing tests stay green without edits, including
689	+  `round_spanning_adjacent_assistant_messages_is_not_split` (two adjacent
690	+  nested messages with no pending results stay two messages).
691	+
692	+## Approaches
693	+
694	+1. **Port the Bedrock condition into the `AssistantMessage` arm, with a shared
695	+   append helper (chosen).** One extra branch before the current flush. It
696	+   matches Bedrock and the `ToolCall` arm, and it is the smallest change.
697	+2. **Turn a tool-use-only message into `Item::ToolCall` items and reuse that
698	+   arm.** This needs a refactor of the loop for no gain, and it makes the two
699	+   translators differ in shape.
700	+3. **Merge every tool-use-only message into the previous assistant message.**
701	+   This changes the wire for well-formed requests (for example, two adjacent
702	+   nested messages), so it breaks the anti-goal.
703	+
704	+## Tests
705	+
706	+Unit tests in the `tests` module of `request.rs`, with Anthropic wire arrays:
707	+
708	+- `nested_tool_use_inside_open_round_joins_it`: the example above gives
709	+  `[utext("q"), assistant[tu(t1), tu(t2), tu(t3)], user[tr(t1), tr(t2), tr(t3)]]`.
710	+- `nested_tool_use_after_answered_round_starts_new_round`:
711	+  `user q, assistant[t1], ToolResult t1, assistant[t2], ToolResult t2` gives
712	+  `[utext("q"), assistant[tu(t1)], user[tr(t1)], assistant[tu(t2)], user[tr(t2)]]`.
713	+- `nested_tool_use_with_reasoning_inside_open_round_joins_it`: the first example
714	+  with `[Reasoning, ToolUse t3]` gives the same array. This proves condition 1
715	+  reads the filtered blocks.
716	+- `nested_tool_use_joins_last_of_adjacent_assistant_run`: a copy of
717	+  `round_spanning_adjacent_assistant_messages_is_not_split` with a nested `t3`
718	+  in place of `call("t3")`. The expected array is the same as in that test.
719	+- `mixed_assistant_inside_open_round_matches_top_level_form`: a parity test.
720	+  The nested form `asst[text b, tu t3]` and the top-level form
721	+  `asst(text b), ToolCall t3`, each inside an open round, give equal output.
722	+  The test asserts the parity, not a specific array. Its doc comment says that
723	+  both forms still give a rejected request (the known gap). Without condition 1
724	+  this test fails.
725	+
726	+Live test: `nested_tool_use_inside_open_round_is_accepted` in
727	+`crates/paigasus-helikon-providers-anthropic/tests/live.rs`, `#[ignore]`, with
728	+`run_accepted` and `toolu_...` ids, as the SMA-819 live tests do. If an API key
729	+is available, one real run goes into the PR body.
730	+
731	+## Docs
732	+
733	+Internal translation fix. The public API, the mdBook, and the crate README do
734	+not change. The code comment of the `AssistantMessage` arm explains the new
735	+branch and the two invariants, as Bedrock's comment does.
